this is nico rosberg  star of formula one racing hard at work  he has a need for speed  and he's looking to clock the fastest  time all in a day's work for the 28 year  old german  but today there's a surprise waiting for  nico  his friend football star lucas padolski  has come to the nurburgring to get a  taste of motor racing  formula one style  in just 4.3 seconds the c63 amg hits the  100 kilometer per hour mark as it heads  out on the legendary north loop track  for lucas it's an adrenaline kick of a  new kind  uh here that's what it's checking here  the c63 amg's road ability is of course  quite different from nico's formula one  car  but it still boasts impressive stats a  maximum  output of 358 kilowatts means everyone's  firmly fixed  in their seats  lucas asked whether niko has to break  hard in this formula one mercedes  nico says oh yeah  time to change places he's got his lucky  shirt on  and now lucas takes the driver's seat  nico's request lucas promises to give it  his all  and he puts his foot right down  nico jokes that lucas could go even  faster on the next curve  but then he has to admit that he's not  the most relaxed passenger  he's been breaking the whole time with  his left foot  nico has passed his first test intact  and now it's on to the next one teammate  lewis hamilton is getting ready for  nico's next challenge  and he's brought along some true racing  legends to do it  these two iconic silver arrows have  returned to the north loop  nico and lewis are going to show how  today's formula 1 stars handle the cars  driven by the racing heroes of  yesteryear  the outdated technology doesn't seem to  hinder either driver  and neither appears worried that the  cars they're driving have a present day  value of several million euros  lewis hamilton is in the older car  marked number two  75 years ago in 1938 britain richard  seaman  won the german grand prix in the w154 at  the nurburgring  16 years later in 1954 argentinian  racing legend juan manuel fangio won the  race  in the eiffel and the w196  and just like today's grand prix the  races drew hundreds of thousands of  spectators  foreign  the 1938 german grand prix was to be a  double victory for mercedes-benz  driving the silver arrows today is  something special for our modern racers  you certainly can't get closer to  history than this  nico says it's great fun to drive the  silver era  but back in the old days there was a  great deal of risk involved in racing  it's incredible to think of the cars  going full throttle on the racing track  and pouring rain without any barriers  between the trees  nico says it's an honor to have driven  the car that fangio won in  it's really great  breaking was a bit of a problem he says  that with the clutch it was tough to  really get a feel for the brakes and the  gears  the car is after all unique  the experience has left an impression on  lewis too  i can't imagine what it was like back  then racing these cars even though i've  done a lap now  um but it's pretty phenomenal what they  did  yeah well the accelerator is in the  middle and the breakers on the right and  then the clutch is on the left  so going down the hill sometimes i was  looking for the brake and couldn't find  it  trying to get into gear is the hardest  thing it doesn't want to go into gear  ever  so it almost stalls each time i was  trying to put it into gear and then you  eventually  crank it into gear but maybe i wasn't  aggressive enough i think i was too  gentle with it  these old-school racing cars may look  tinny but they're far from being  consigned to the scrap heap  our young stars have shown that the  silver arrows are still worthy of  respect as true makers of racing history  you
 

